{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall set up for yourself stones.</b> Rashi explains: you would thereby say that there were three sets of stones — twelve in the Jordan, and corresponding to them at Gilgal, and corresponding to them on Mount Eival, as stated in tractate Sotah. One may object, for in tractate Sotah the other version implies: you would thereby say that there were three sets of stones — one that Moses set up in the plains of Moab, as it is said, 'Moses undertook to expound' — expound, expound; and one that Joshua set up at Gilgal — they are the very same ones from which they built an altar on Mount Eival, plastered it with lime, wrote the Torah upon it, brought burnt offerings and peace offerings upon it, ate, rejoiced, blessed and cursed; then they folded the stones and came and lodged at Gilgal, as it is said: 'and you shall pass them with you and set them down at the lodging where you lodge tonight,' and it is written: 'these twelve stones which they took from the Jordan, Joshua set up at Gilgal.'" "<b>Large stones.</b> To accommodate the writing of the reckoning of the commandments of the Torah, as they are written in the Halakhot Gedolot in the matter of the warnings." "<b>And you shall plaster them with lime.</b> To lay the lime between row and row, so as to join them one to another, in the manner of building with other stones." "<b>[with lime.</b> A noun-form of lime (סיד).]"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:54:27.031161502Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}